@using BlazorDLR.Shared.State
@inject StartupCheckState Startup

@*
	The wall (§20.1). MainLayout renders this INSTEAD OF @Body, which is what makes it a wall
	rather than a dialog: there is no route underneath it to reach, so nothing behind it can make
	a call the server would refuse.

	The nav rail stays. A rider who cannot use the app can still read what is wrong, and hiding
	the furniture as well would look like a crash rather than a decision.

	Nothing here calls the server. This build is one the server has already said it will not
	serve, so a retry button would be a button that cannot work.
*@

<div class="page page-narrow">
	<div class="wall">
		<h1><i class="fa fa-fw fa-circle-arrow-up" aria-hidden="true"></i> Time for an update</h1>

		<p>
			This copy of Dumb Luck Routes is too old to talk to the server properly. Rather than let
			it half work and lose something you have recorded, it stops here.
		</p>

		<p class="wall-versions">
			This app is version <strong>@(StartupCheckState.ClientVersion ?? "unknown")</strong>.
			The server needs at least <strong>@Startup.MinimumVersion</strong>.
		</p>

		@if (Startup.UpdateUrl is { } store)
		{
			<p><a class="button" href="@store" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get the new version</a></p>
		}
		else
		{
			<p>Update the app from the store you installed it from, then open it again.</p>
		}
	</div>
</div>

<style>
	.wall {
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border); border-top: 4px solid var(--dlr-warn); border-radius: 8px;
		background: var(--dlr-surface);
		padding: 1.5rem 1.25rem;
		margin-top: 2rem;
	}
	.wall h1 { margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.3rem; }
	.wall h1 i { color: var(--dlr-warn); }
	.wall p { margin: 0 0 1rem; line-height: 1.5; }
	.wall p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
	.wall-versions { color: var(--dlr-fg-muted); }
</style>
